1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,187
This is MATH 239, video 16 b. The topic is the Main Theorem, from Section 4.3.1 of the course notes. It uses partial fractions from video 16 a.

2
00:00:14,637 --> 00:00:33,282
After this video, you can handle a numerator of large degree by division. You can state and prove the Main Theorem. You can go from a recurrence straight to a formula. And you can solve Example 4.15 and notes exercises 4.4 and 4.5.

3
00:00:33,732 --> 00:00:51,972
We start with a review of partial fractions from video 16 a: notes exercise 4.4. Pause the video and try it. The denominator is the same as in Example 4.13. Only the numerator changes: x plus 7 x squared.

4
00:00:52,422 --> 00:01:13,862
Part a. By Theorem 4.8, the denominator gives the recurrence: a n minus 3 a n minus 2, minus 2 a n minus 3, equals 0 for n at least 3. The numerator gives a 0 equals 0, a 1 equals 1, and a 2 minus 3 a 0 equals 7, so a 2 equals 7.

5
00:01:14,312 --> 00:01:35,752
Part b. Use the same form as before, and substitute. At x equals minus 1, 3 B equals 6, so B equals 2. At x equals one half, nine quarters C equals nine quarters, so C equals 1. At x equals 0, A plus B plus C equals 0, so A equals minus 3.

6
00:01:36,202 --> 00:01:48,831
Expand each term with binomial series. a n equals minus 3 times minus 1 to the n, plus 2 times n plus 1 times minus 1 to the n, plus 2 to the n.

7
00:01:49,281 --> 00:02:10,636
Collect the terms: a n equals 2n minus 1, times minus 1 to the n, plus 2 to the n. Check: n equals 0 gives minus 1 plus 1, which is 0. n equals 3 gives minus 5 plus 8, which is 3. The values 0, 1, 7, 3, 23 agree with the recurrence.

8
00:02:11,086 --> 00:02:27,470
Theorem 4.12 needs the degree of P to be less than the degree of Q. Here is a series where this fails. We meet it again in Example 4.15. The numerator has degree 5. The denominator has degree 3.

9
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:46,053
First divide the polynomials. H equals R plus P over Q, where P has degree less than 3. R is the quotient. Its degree is 5 minus 3, which is 2. The fractions are not nice, but the method is the same as long division of numbers.

10
00:02:46,503 --> 00:03:02,994
R of x is a polynomial. It only changes the coefficients of x to the 0, x, and x squared. The orange cells show them. P over Q satisfies Theorem 4.12, so it has a partial fraction expansion.

11
00:03:03,444 --> 00:03:21,279
Add the two rows. The sum is the series H: 1, 1, 0, 2, minus 4, 3. From x cubed on, the coefficients of H and of P over Q are equal. So a formula from P over Q holds for all n greater than the degree of R.

12
00:03:21,729 --> 00:03:39,435
Now the Main Theorem, Theorem 4.14. Assume G of x equals a polynomial R, plus P over Q, with the degree of P less than the degree of Q, and constant term of Q equal to 1. Factor Q into its inverse roots.

13
00:03:39,885 --> 00:03:53,027
In the example, Q equals one minus 2x, squared, times one plus x. The inverse root 2 has multiplicity 2. The inverse root minus 1 has multiplicity 1.

14
00:03:53,477 --> 00:04:14,042
Each inverse root lambda i contributes a polynomial p i of n, times lambda i to the n. The degree of p i is less than the multiplicity d i. Here, 2 gives A plus B n, times 2 to the n. Minus 1 gives a constant C, times minus 1 to the n.

15
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:28,209
So g n equals A plus B n, times 2 to the n, plus C times minus 1 to the n. This holds for all n greater than the degree of R. We do not need to find the partial fractions.

16
00:04:28,659 --> 00:04:52,681
Why is the Main Theorem true? R only changes the coefficients up to its degree, so we can ignore it. Apply Theorem 4.12 to P over Q. The factor one minus lambda i x, to the power d i, gives d i terms. Expand each term with the binomial series. Every term has the same power lambda i to the n.

17
00:04:53,131 --> 00:05:10,901
So the contribution is lambda i to the n, times a sum of binomial coefficients n plus j minus 1 choose j minus 1. For j equal to 1, this is 1. For j equal to 2, it is n plus 1. The rows show their values.

18
00:05:11,351 --> 00:05:25,666
For j equal to 3, it is n plus 2 times n plus 1, over 2. In general, n plus j minus 1 choose j minus 1 is a polynomial in n of degree j minus 1.

19
00:05:26,116 --> 00:05:44,697
The largest j is d i. So p i of n is a polynomial of degree at most d i minus 1. Add the contributions of all the inverse roots. This gives the formula of the theorem. The converse is also true. It is Exercise 4.11.

20
00:05:45,147 --> 00:06:01,766
With the Main Theorem, we can go from a recurrence straight to a formula. Step 1: Theorem 4.8 gives Q of x from the coefficients of the recurrence. Step 2: factor Q to find the inverse roots and their multiplicities.

21
00:06:02,216 --> 00:06:24,531
Step 3: write the general form, a sum of polynomials times powers. Step 4: find where it holds. If the recurrence holds for n at least N, the numerator has degree at most N minus 1. So the degree of R is at most N minus 1 minus d. The formula holds for n at least N minus d.

22
00:06:24,981 --> 00:06:36,138
Step 5: the general form has d unknown constants. Take d values of g n with n at least N minus d. Solve the linear equations.

23
00:06:36,588 --> 00:06:56,172
Example 4.15. Pause the video and try it. Six initial values are given: 1, 1, 0, 2, minus 4, 3. The recurrence h n minus 3 h n minus 1, plus 4 h n minus 3, equals 0 holds for n at least 6.

24
00:06:56,622 --> 00:07:15,118
The recurrence puts weights on four neighbouring terms: 1 on h n, minus 3 on h n minus 1, 0 on h n minus 2, and 4 on h n minus 3. At n equals 6, this gives h 6 equals 1. The gray cells continue the sequence.

25
00:07:15,568 --> 00:07:25,317
By Theorem 4.8, these weights are the coefficients of the denominator. Q of x equals one minus 3x plus 4 x cubed.

26
00:07:25,767 --> 00:07:41,341
Factor Q. At x equals minus 1, Q is 1 plus 3 minus 4, which is 0. So one plus x is a factor. The quotient is one minus 4x plus 4 x squared, which is one minus 2x, squared.

27
00:07:41,791 --> 00:07:59,967
By the Main Theorem, h n equals A plus B n, times 2 to the n, plus C times minus 1 to the n. The recurrence holds from N equals 6, and d equals 3. So the degree of R is at most 2, and the formula holds for n at least 3.

28
00:08:00,417 --> 00:08:19,681
Use the data from n equals 3, 4 and 5. Put each value into the form. For n equals 3, 2 to the 3 is 8 and minus 1 cubed is minus 1. This gives 8 A plus 24 B minus C equals 2. The other two equations come the same way.

29
00:08:20,131 --> 00:08:33,165
Solve the three equations. The notes give A equals minus 5 over 16, B equals 1 over 16, and C equals minus 3. Substitute them back to check each equation.

30
00:08:33,615 --> 00:08:38,821
Here are the values of the formula next to h n, for n from 0 to 8.

31
00:08:39,271 --> 00:08:57,553
From n equals 3 on, they agree: 2, minus 4, 3, 1, 19, 45. For n from 0 to 2 they do not agree. The formula gives the coefficients of P over Q. R changes exactly these three values, as we saw before.

32
00:08:58,003 --> 00:09:21,726
A plus B n is n minus 5 over 16. Times 2 to the n, this is n minus 5, times 2 to the n minus 4. So h n equals n minus 5 times 2 to the n minus 4, minus 3 times minus 1 to the n, for n at least 3. The values for n from 0 to 2 are the initial conditions.

33
00:09:22,176 --> 00:09:45,451
Exercise 4.5 gives no hint for the factors. Pause the video and try it. Part a: by Theorem 4.8, the denominator gives c n minus 4 c n minus 1, plus 5 c n minus 2, minus 2 c n minus 3, equals 0 for n at least 3. The initial values are 3, 1 and 0.

34
00:09:45,901 --> 00:09:54,285
Part b. Test x equals 1: 1 minus 4 plus 5 minus 2 is 0. So one minus x is a factor.

35
00:09:54,735 --> 00:10:08,836
Divide Q by one minus x. The quotient is one minus 3x plus 2 x squared. The grid multiplies the two factors back. Each cell is one product, coloured by its power of x.

36
00:10:09,286 --> 00:10:22,342
Add each colour: 1, then minus 3 minus 1 equals minus 4, then 2 plus 3 equals 5, then minus 2. These are the coefficients of Q, so the division is correct.

37
00:10:22,792 --> 00:10:36,531
The quotient factors again: one minus 3x plus 2 x squared equals one minus x, times one minus 2x. So Q equals one minus x, squared, times one minus 2x.

38
00:10:36,981 --> 00:10:58,335
The inverse root 1 has multiplicity 2, and 2 has multiplicity 1. By the Main Theorem, c n equals A plus B n, times 1 to the n, plus C times 2 to the n. The numerator has degree 2, less than 3, so R is zero. The formula holds for all n, starting at 0.

39
00:10:58,785 --> 00:11:11,756
Use c 0, c 1 and c 2. This gives three equations: A plus C equals 3, A plus B plus 2 C equals 1, and A plus 2 B plus 4 C equals 0.

40
00:11:12,206 --> 00:11:27,225
Subtract the first equation from the second: B plus C equals minus 2. Subtract the second from the third: B plus 2 C equals minus 1. So C equals 1, B equals minus 3, and A equals 2.

41
00:11:27,675 --> 00:11:43,376
Check with the rows. 2 minus 3 n gives 2, minus 1, minus 4, and so on. 2 to the n gives 1, 2, 4. The sum is 3, 1, 0, 1, 6, 19, 48: the coefficients of the series.

42
00:11:43,826 --> 00:11:50,247
The answer: c n equals 2 minus 3 n plus 2 to the n, for all n at least 0.

43
00:11:50,697 --> 00:12:14,356
Go back to exercise 4.4, and use the Main Theorem instead. The inverse root 2 has multiplicity 1. The inverse root minus 1 has multiplicity 2. The numerator has degree 2, less than 3, so R is zero. So a n equals C times 2 to the n, plus A plus B n, times minus 1 to the n, for all n.

44
00:12:14,806 --> 00:12:35,051
Fit the constants to a 0 equals 0, a 1 equals 1, and a 2 equals 7. The first equation gives A equals minus C. Put this into the other two: B equals 3 C minus 1, and 9 C equals 9. So C equals 1, A equals minus 1, and B equals 2.

45
00:12:35,501 --> 00:12:49,944
Check with the rows. 2 to the n, plus 2n minus 1 times minus 1 to the n, gives 0, 1, 7, 3, 23, 23, 75. These are the coefficients of A of x.

46
00:12:50,394 --> 00:13:04,474
The answer is the same as with partial fractions: a n equals 2 to the n, plus 2n minus 1 times minus 1 to the n. The Main Theorem saves the work of finding the partial fractions.

47
00:13:04,924 --> 00:13:23,783
To recap. If the degree of P is not less than the degree of Q, divide first. R changes only the first few coefficients. The Main Theorem gives the form of g n: a polynomial times a power for each inverse root, for n greater than the degree of R.

48
00:13:24,233 --> 00:13:40,531
If the recurrence holds from N on and has order d, the formula holds from N minus d on. Fit the d constants with d values, and check against the sequence. The next video covers inhomogeneous and quadratic recurrences.
